  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.5 x² + 0.5 z²)/2, drawn at half its value over x, z in [-2, 2]. The x direction is 5 times steeper, so a learning rate above 0.8 makes x swings grow there.",
    "Each step moves the ball by −learning rate × (true slope + noise). Noise sd = 1.5·√((64−b)/(63·b)) per direction, centred on zero, exactly zero at b = 64. One update every 0.2 s while playing.",
    "Both arrows are drawn at 2× their true step length (the same scale). The orange arrow is the next actual move, against the noisy slope; the grey arrow is against the true slope. The length readout gives the true value.",
    "A run stops after 40 steps (finished) or when the loss exceeds 8 (diverged). The ball and trail are capped at the bowl's edge on screen; the loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "Noise comes from the fixed seed, the run number and the step, so Reset replays the same sequence and each new run uses different noise. Changing a setting mid-run affects the following steps."
  ]
}